{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1794662837,"nonce":"OpoUazmDvQZ+4MfGKnoQNHOrA1I=","message":"Persistent cache, as TAST layers. Committed locally (6a8ce324, 4e051964, 0221aacc), not pushed.\n\nDesign. A file is a layer of a stack: the standard library, then a library, then the program. A layer keeps its own plain ids; a reference into a lower layer is a tagged id (top bit, then the layer's position, then the index), allowed only for types, classes, fields, abstracts, enums and enum constructors, so the exporter's own tables did not change. A layer carries an identity, the identities of its parents, a key index (how the exporter found each type, class, field and enum by name), a digest of its source files, the defines it was built with and the names its sources test in #if, and, in a program, the entries dead-code removal kept from the layers below. `Stack::parse` reads the files together, refuses a layer not built on the ones below it, and checks every tagged id against the layer it names. `Dumper` prints a function with names for ids, and `tast_dump --against=whole.tast` checks that a stack has the same functions with the same text as the program exported whole.\n\nBuilding: `scripts/std_layer.sh OUT.tast -D hl-ver=... [-D analyzer-optimize ...]` builds the std layer; it excludes, one at a time, the modules the compiler cannot type under those defines (haxe.atomic below HL 1.13, haxe.macro.ExampleJSGenerator), then runs `Export.init(out, [], [\"\u003cstd\u003e\"])` with `-dce no`. A library layer is `Export.init(out, [parents...], [library roots])` with `include('\u003cpackage\u003e')` and `-lib`, built on the layers it needs. A program is `Export.init(out, [layers...])` with its normal build.\n\nA layer is ignored, with a warning, and the program exported whole, when: a source file under its roots changed, a define it was built with differs, the program sets a define the layer's sources test or the compiler types by (analyzer*, no-inline, ...), or a class it holds comes from other sources. Defines the sources never test are free: the unit suite's utest, message.reporting and so on do not matter. The analyzer matters: `-dce full` with `-D analyzer-optimize` rewrites bodies before a macro sees them, so a layer for such programs is built with the same flag.\n\nChecked, not assumed. Tests drive the real compiler: a program on the std layer has exactly the functions, with the same text, as the program exported whole, with and without the analyzer, with an unrelated define, across three layers (std, a library, a program), and a changed library source or a define the std tests makes the layer be ignored. At scale: the Haxe unit suite on std (8,902 functions) and on std plus utest, and Ashui's smoke test (5,063 functions) on a std layer built with its HL 1.12 defines: missing 0, extra 0, differing 0 each time (the unit suite's build-time timestamp string aside).\n\nMeasured (macro-side time added to a compile, medians of 6 runs on a loaded machine):\n\n    program                  whole      on layers      nodes whole -\u003e on layers\n    hello world              228 ms     54 ms          26.6k -\u003e 45\n    Haxe unit suite          2452 ms    1830 ms (std)  334k -\u003e 272k (std), 254k (std+utest)\n                                        1806 ms (std+utest)\n    Ashui smoke              1780 ms    1530 ms        215k -\u003e 175k\n\nThe cost that remains is the program's own code and the stock typecheck (2.5 s for the unit suite), which the layers do not touch. Std is 15-30% of the nodes of an application and utest 5%; the cache pays most for a program that is mostly library code, and for a library layer (a stable Ashui for the apps built on it). Typing itself is only cached by the compiler's server (`haxe --wait`/`--connect`); with it, the exporter's share becomes the larger one and the layers matter more.\n\nLimits: the check on defines reads `#if` and `#elseif` only, not `Context.defined` inside std macros; a layer is valid for one set of typing-relevant defines, so the driver builds one per set; type parameters with the same name and structure share an id; the source digest uses file times, so touching a file rebuilds the layer; the type of a reference to a class (the structure of its statics) is not compared, since dead-code removal prunes it. Not done: the driver that decides which layers a build needs and builds them when they are missing or stale; library layers need the same exclusion search as std (utest needs utest.MacroRunner excluded); the Rust side that lowers a stack, which must honour `Kept`.","files":null}]}